### What are `findFirst` and `findAny`?

Both are terminal operations on a stream that return an `Optional`. They pick a single element that survived the pipeline, or an empty Optional if none did. Both are short-circuiting, meaning they stop traversing as soon as they can produce a result.

The difference is the ordering contract. `findFirst` respects encounter order and returns the first matching element. `findAny` is free to return any matching element.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import com.bank.model.Employee;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class FindDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = List.of(
                new Employee(1, "Rajesh", 2008, "Trading", new BigDecimal("1200000")),
                new Employee(2, "Priya", 2011, "Risk", new BigDecimal("850000")),
                new Employee(3, "Pankaj", 2015, "Trading", new BigDecimal("990000"))
        );

        Optional<Employee> first = employees.stream()
                .filter(e -> e.department().equals("Trading"))
                .findFirst();

        first.ifPresent(e -> System.out.println("findFirst -> " + e.name()));
    }
}
```

```text
findFirst -> Rajesh
```

The `Employee` record:

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record Employee(long id, String name, int year, String department, BigDecimal salary) { }
```

### How do you fetch the first match on a sorted stream?

To get the longest-serving Trading employee, sort by joining year ascending and take `findFirst`. On an ordered stream the result is deterministic and repeatable.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import com.bank.model.Employee;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class LongestServing {
    public Optional<Employee> longestServingIn(List<Employee> employees, String department) {
        return employees.stream()
                .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::year))
                .filter(e -> e.department().equals(department))
                .findFirst();
    }
}
```

```text
// For the sample data, department "Trading":
Optional[Employee[id=1, name=Rajesh, year=2008, ...]]
```

Because 2008 is the earliest joining year among Trading employees, `findFirst` returns Rajesh every single run.

### What is the encounter-order guarantee?

Encounter order is the order defined by the stream source. A `List` has a defined order, and `sorted` imposes one. An ordered stream carries this order through the pipeline. `findFirst` honors it and returns the element that comes first in that order. `findAny` ignores it.

```java
// Ordered source: findFirst is deterministic
Optional<Integer> a = List.of(10, 20, 30, 40).stream()
        .filter(n -> n > 15)
        .findFirst(); // always Optional[20]

// Unordered source (a HashSet has no encounter order)
Optional<Integer> b = new java.util.HashSet<>(List.of(10, 20, 30, 40)).stream()
        .filter(n -> n > 15)
        .findFirst(); // first in iteration order, which is unspecified
```

### How do they behave in a parallel stream?

In parallel, the stream is split across threads. `findFirst` must still return the element earliest in encounter order, so threads coordinate: a match found by a later segment cannot win over a match in an earlier segment. That coordination costs some performance. `findAny` has no such obligation, so any thread that finds a match can win immediately, which is why it is cheaper in parallel.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class ParallelFindDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int first = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 1_000_000)
                .parallel()
                .filter(n -> n % 7 == 0)
                .findFirst()
                .orElse(-1);
        // Deterministic: always 7, the earliest multiple of 7
        System.out.println("findFirst -> " + first);

        int any = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 1_000_000)
                .parallel()
                .filter(n -> n % 7 == 0)
                .findAny()
                .orElse(-1);
        // Any multiple of 7 a thread finds first; may vary run to run
        System.out.println("findAny -> " + any);
    }
}
```

```text
findFirst -> 7
findAny -> 7       (or 490007, or some other multiple of 7, depending on the run)
```

### How do `findFirst` and `findAny` compare?

| Aspect | `findFirst` | `findAny` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Returns | first in encounter order | any matching element |
| Determinism (ordered stream) | guaranteed | not guaranteed |
| Sequential behavior | first element | usually first (not guaranteed) |
| Parallel cost | higher (coordination) | lower (no coordination) |
| Result type | `Optional<T>` | `Optional<T>` |
| Short-circuits | yes | yes |

```java
// Side by side on the same sorted pipeline
Optional<Employee> deterministic = employees.stream()
        .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::year))
        .filter(e -> e.department().equals("Trading"))
        .findFirst();

Optional<Employee> fast = employees.parallelStream()
        .filter(e -> e.department().equals("Trading"))
        .findAny();
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is relying on `findAny` to return the first element because it happens to do so sequentially.

```java
// WRONG: assuming findAny returns the earliest match
Employee longestServing = employees.parallelStream()
        .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::year))
        .filter(e -> e.department().equals("Trading"))
        .findAny() // may return any Trading employee, not the earliest
        .orElseThrow();
```

```java
// CORRECT: use findFirst when identity matters
Employee longestServing = employees.stream()
        .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::year))
        .filter(e -> e.department().equals("Trading"))
        .findFirst()
        .orElseThrow();
```

The second mistake is calling `get()` on the returned Optional without handling emptiness, which throws `NoSuchElementException` when no element matches.

```java
// WRONG: get on a possibly-empty result
Employee e = employees.stream().filter(x -> x.year() == 1900).findFirst().get();
// NoSuchElementException
```

```java
// CORRECT: handle the empty case
Employee e = employees.stream().filter(x -> x.year() == 1900).findFirst()
        .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalStateException("no employee joined in 1900"));
```

### What are edge cases and senior nuances?

On a sequential stream, `findAny` is usually implemented to return the first element, but the contract does not guarantee it, so you must not depend on that. The empty case for both is a clean empty Optional, never null, so there is no `NullPointerException` from the find itself.

Another nuance: if you only need to know whether a match exists, prefer `anyMatch`, which returns a boolean and does not build an Optional.

```java
// Only existence matters -> anyMatch, not findAny
boolean hasTrading = employees.stream().anyMatch(e -> e.department().equals("Trading"));
```

Calling `unordered()` on a stream before `findFirst` tells the pipeline to drop the ordering guarantee, which can let the runtime treat `findFirst` like `findAny` for better parallel performance when you truly do not care about order.

```java
Optional<Employee> representative = employees.parallelStream()
        .unordered()
        .filter(e -> e.department().equals("Trading"))
        .findFirst(); // now free to behave like findAny
```

### When should you prefer each?

Choose `findFirst` when the identity of the result matters, for example the earliest transaction, the longest-serving employee for a loyalty award, or the top-ranked record. Choose `findAny` when you only need existence or any representative and want maximum parallel speed, for example a health check that confirms at least one employee exists in a department.

```java
// Identity matters: deterministic winner for a loyalty award
Optional<Employee> award = employees.stream()
        .filter(e -> e.department().equals("Trading"))
        .min(Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::year));

// Only existence matters: any representative, fastest in parallel
Optional<Employee> sample = employees.parallelStream()
        .filter(e -> e.department().equals("Trading"))
        .findAny();
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers often ask whether `findFirst` is deterministic in parallel, where the answer is yes on an ordered stream, at a coordination cost. They ask why `findAny` exists, where the answer is to avoid that coordination and run faster in parallel. They ask what both return on no match, where the answer is an empty `Optional`. And they ask the difference between `findAny` and `anyMatch`, where the answer is `findAny` returns the element in an Optional and `anyMatch` returns a boolean.
